// eqnum.spp -- optional infix synonyms for the eqnum/neqnum escape hatch
// (see preprocess/eqrewrite.cfp's own "Handmatige ontsnappingsklep"
// section): `x ~~ y` / `x <> y` instead of `eqnum x y` / `neqnum x y`.
//
// #import this file to get the infix form. `eqnum`/`neqnum` themselves
// need NO import -- they're recognized directly by eqrewrite.cfp as a
// bare, saturated 2-argument call, not real functions. This file's own
// `(~~)`/`(<>)` bodies use `eqnum`/`neqnum` the same way, so they get
// rewritten to a raw `==`/`!=` too (same near-zero cost, plus the one
// ordinary function-call this wrapper itself adds).
//
// Sapl+-only (needs the driver.jmvm preprocessing step) -- meaningless to
// #import into a hand-written .cfp compiled directly via ./compile.
//
// Symbol choice: Sapl+'s user-operator lexer (preprocess/lexer.cfp's
// isUserOpCh) only accepts `+ - * / % < > & ~ ^ $` -- `=`/`!` are
// deliberately excluded (they keep their single-char meaning always), so
// a literal `=n=`/`!n=` (or anything mimicking `==`/`!=`'s own shape)
// isn't lexable at all. `~~` (doubled tilde, "approximately/raw equals")
// and `<>` (the classic Pascal/SQL "not equal") are both valid
// isUserOpCh runs and don't collide with any reserved 2/3-char pair
// (==, !=, <=, >=, /\, \/, ->, <-, :=, <#>).
//
// Deliberately declared at a LOW priority (3, matching the built-in `==`/
// `!=`'s own conceptual level) -- though note preprocess/parser.cfp's own
// `#operator` comment: a user-declared operator ALWAYS binds tighter than
// every built-in regardless of the N you pick (userOpInternalPrio maps N
// through `2000 - N`, so declared operators never interleave with
// built-ins on priority alone) -- parenthesize if you mix `~~`/`<>` with
// `==`/`+`/etc. in one expression.

#operator l 3 ~~
#operator l 3 <>

(~~) a b = eqnum a b
(<>) a b = neqnum a b
